Title: Curiosity: The Urge to Explore the Unknown
Date: March 7, 2026
Tag: Curiosity

<style>
  strong {
    font-weight: bold;
  }
</style>

<p>Curiosity is the desire to know or understand something you don’t know yet.  
It’s the feeling or drive that makes you ask “Why?”, “How?”, or “What happens if…?”</p>

<blockquote>Curiosity is the urge to explore the unknown.</blockquote>

<p>It pushes us to:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Ask questions</li>
  <li>Investigate things</li>
  <li>Learn new ideas</li>
  <li>Explore patterns and mysteries</li>
</ul>

<p>Curiosity does not require you to be highly intellectual or deeply passionate. Anyone can be curious.  
Although passion may ignite or keep curiosity alive, it is not necessary for curiosity to exist.  
You can be curious about many things without being passionate about them or an expert in them.</p>

<p>At its core, curiosity is simply the ability and willingness to ask questions, even simple ones such as:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Why is the sky blue?</li>
  <li>Why is grass green?</li>
  <li>Why is water wet?</li>
</ul>

<blockquote>Curiosity is not about already knowing things — it is about wanting to know.</blockquote>
